  <exercise workspace="1.5in">
    <introduction><p>
For a standard calculus antidifferentiation problem, <m>f</m> is a function of <m>t</m> only, and <m>\frac{dy}{dt}=f(t)</m>, e.g. <m>\frac{dy}{dt}=t^2</m>. In such situations, the slopes of the arrows in the slope field
are constant on ...(you complete the rest).
</p></introduction>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1.5in">
    <introduction><p>
For an autonomous, first-order differential equation, <m>f</m> is a function of <m>y</m> only, and <m>\frac{dy}{dt}=f(t)</m>, e.g. <m>\frac{dy}{dt}=2-y</m>. 
In such situations, the slopes of the arrows in the slope field
are constant on ...(you complete the rest).

</p></introduction>
  </exercise>

